  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative expert interviews across three specific European case studies, which may not represent the institutional dynamics of first-tier metropolitan areas or different national welfare regimes.",
      "what": "This study analyzes local social inclusion policies for migrants in three second-tier European cities—Cluj-Napoca (Romania), Bologna (Italy), and Halle/Saale (Germany)—using expert interviews. The research evaluates how these municipalities adapt to migration-related heterogeneity, fund services, and target vulnerable mobile EU citizens, finding that local efforts are constrained by outsourcing to NGOs and short-term policy interventions.",
      "why": "It demonstrates how fragmented multi-level governance and historical path dependencies actively preserve power inequalities and limit municipal capacity, showing that local integration efforts cannot be understood in isolation from supra-local constraints."
    }
  },
